    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots into the existing validation conventions exactly, with nothing duplicated or misplaced:\n\n- **Right unit / right pattern.** The codebase already pairs a `*Validation` annotation with a `*Validator` (`TelephoneValidation`/`TelephoneValidator`, `PetAgeValidation`/`PetAgeValidator`, `PostcodeValidation`/`PostcodeValidator`) in `rest/validation`. `EmailDomainValidation`/`EmailDomainValidator` follows that shape precisely \u2014 field-level `@Target(FIELD)`, `@Constraint(validatedBy = ...)`, the standard `message`/`groups`/`payload` trio.\n- **Consistent wiring.** It's attached via `x-field-extra-annotation` on the `email` field in `openapi.yml`, identical to how `TelephoneValidation`, `PetAgeValidation`, and `PastOrPresent` are wired. No controller/service/mapper changes were smuggled in \u2014 a domain blocklist is genuinely just a field constraint, so keeping it out of the service layer is the natural choice here.\n- **Idiomatic pass-through.** The `null`/blank short-circuit (\"absence and syntax handled elsewhere\") mirrors the exact convention in `PostcodeValidator` and `TelephoneValidation`'s docs, so a maintainer would find the \"return true for absent\" behavior unsurprising rather than a gap.\n- **No catch-all growth.** The blocklist lives as a private `Set` inside its own single-purpose validator rather than being bolted onto an existing class.\n\nNothing is becoming a grab-bag and no logic is duplicated. The structure is already clean.",
